id:                     DRAFT-ohos-shell-ulimit-capability-hops
trigger_tool:           interpreter|lisp|prolog|scheme|make|autotools|wrapper|test_package
phase:                  building|testing|consumer-run
error_signature:        Signal 11 \(core dumped\)|SIGSEGV.*deep recursion|Error 139|stack overflow
severity:               2
category:               ohos, shell-capability, ulimit, stack, hmdfs, interpreter
match_keywords:         ulimit, mksh, hnp bash, stack, Signal 11, GC, recursion, wrapper, no-op, 跳板
title:                  OHOS shell ulimit capability differs (mksh silent no-op); stack escalation must land on every exec hop via a capability-tested shell
root_cause:             OHOS device default main-thread stack (8MB) is too small for
                        deep C recursion inside old interpreters (GC mark phase,
                        compiler recursion). ulimit is a process attribute that only
                        inherits downward, so raising it once is insufficient; and
                        /system/bin/sh (mksh) implements ulimit -s as a silent no-op
                        (empty query output, rc=0), while hnp bash applies it for real.
rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           capability-probe each candidate shell for ulimit -s, then land
                        the raise on every exec hop: build step scripts, the installed
                        wrapper script, and the consumer test harness; validate by
                        repeat-count crash rate (>=10 runs) on exit codes, not on
                        captured output (SEGV drops stdio buffers).
rec_command:            ulimit -s unlimited 2>/dev/null || ulimit -s 65536 2>/dev/null || true
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               conan-recipe-step-scripts-and-wrapper-patch-and-test-harness
fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      skip-if-no-capability
fallback_description:   If no capable shell exists on the device, record the crash as a
                        platform hard limit with the 20/20-vs-N/N evidence pair; do not
                        claim a fix from a silent no-op.
forbid_1_pattern:       ulimit -s unlimited 2>/dev/null \|{1,2} true
forbid_1_reason:        The `|| true` fallback hides the mksh silent no-op; the raise
                        looks applied but the limit is unchanged.
forbid_1_consequence:   Intermittent/deterministic SIGSEGV survives every "fixed" run;
                        the failure gets misattributed to the wrong root cause.
forbid_2_pattern:       grep.*Signal 11.*\|\| (ok|pass)
forbid_2_reason:        Counting crashes by absence of captured "Signal 11" text is
                        unreliable: SEGV drops buffered stdio, and instrumented inputs
                        (polluted .orig restore chains) produce false negatives.
forbid_2_consequence:   False pass evidence; polluted shared inputs keep being re-tested.
affected_scope:         OHOS/HarmonyOS PC native builds and consumer tests of deep-C-
                        recursion programs (Lisp/Prolog/Smalltalk interpreters, macro
                        expanders, recursive-descent parsers, self-hosting compilers);
                        any ulimit escalation under multi-shell environments (mksh + hnp bash).
verification_cmd:       run the failing input >=10 times via each candidate shell with
                        `ulimit -s` query before/after; count nonzero exits by pipeline
                        exit code only
example_evidence:       xlispstat 3.52.23 r14-r25: 20/20 SEGV on default stack -> 10/10
                        clean with hnp bash ulimit -s unlimited; fixes landed in three
                        hops (make step scripts, 0001 wrapper patch, test harness).
knowledge_ref:          E087, DRAFT-cdb-20251021-hmdfs-shebang-script-exec-refusal,
                        DRAFT-gcc-snapshot-20260705-ohos-source-tree-and-fresh-build
dedup_statement:        ulimit appears only as an aside in the gcc-snapshot draft; no
                        existing entry covers the mksh silent no-op, the capability
                        probe, or the three-hop completeness rule. Shebang script-exec
                        refusal is covered by DRAFT-cdb-20251021 and is not repeated here.
